                <pb facs="0093" n="83" sameAs="frus:frus1941v06/pg83"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Pacific War/766: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Costa Rica</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Lane</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">San
                                José</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-12-08T15:00:00-06:00">December 8, 1941—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 8:34 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>378. Department’s circular telegram December 8, 1 a.m. The Military Attaché has
                    been informed by high Government officials that all radio sets owned by German
                    nationals will be sequestered. The Legation has also advised Tropical Radio, All
                    America Cables and Pan-American Airways that they must exert the utmost
                    vigilance to prevent enemy parties from taking advantage of present facilities
                    for interests inimical to the United States.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lane</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
